WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en
Style:
::cue(c.colorCCCCCC) { color: rgb(204,204,204);
 }
::cue(c.colorEEEEEE) { color: rgb(238,238,238);
 }
::cue(c.cyan) { color: cyan; }
::cue(c.yellow) { color: yellow; }
##

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.040 line:91%
The technical term
for these things is

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.320 line:85%
'transmission towers'.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.720 line:91%
But here in Britain,
we call them pylons.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.920 line:91%
And we've got tens of thousands
of them across the country.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.160 line:91%
The first National Grid was
built in the interwar years,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.320 line:91%
and it was the largest
peacetime construction project

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.600 line:85%
Britain had ever seen.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.960 line:91%
A single power network
across the country.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.680 line:91%
<i>Pylons became part of
the landscape of Britain,</i>

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.160 line:91%
<i>just as they had
around the world,</i>

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.360 line:85%
<i>despite the objections</i>

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.240 line:91%
of many landowners,
traditionalists, and poets.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.280 line:91%
These exist because
overhead lines are

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.360 line:91%
a fraction of the price
of underground cables,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.000 line:91%
and they're much, much easier
and faster to maintain.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.760 line:91%
If there's a fault,
you don't need to bring in

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.560 line:91%
heavy equipment and
dig anything up.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.120 line:91%
Instead, you dispatch
a team to climb.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.360 line:91%
And that team will have
learned the job here,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.360 line:91%
<i>at the National Grid
Training Centre</i>

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.080 line:91%
<i>near Eakring in
the East Midlands.</i>

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.360 line:91%
And today,
one of those teams

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.640 line:91%
is going to take me
up on the wires.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.000 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>So here at
the Training Centre,</i></c>

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.680 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>we have over 300
graduates and apprentices</i></c>

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.240 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>training in overhead lines
and substation technologies.</i></c>

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.640 line:91%
<c.yellow>The team that
have trained here</c>

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.000 line:85%
<c.yellow>will undertake a range of roles.</c>

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.280 line:91%
<c.yellow>They might be overhead
line operators,</c>

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.120 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>so they'll be climbing towers,
responding to faults.</i></c>

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.160 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>They also conduct
visual inspections.</i></c>

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.560 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>We have a team of drone
and helicopter pilots</i></c>

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.600 line:91%
<c.yellow>that keep the
network operating.</c>

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.720 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>And then we have
substation teams as well.</i></c>

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.040 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>So they would look at
cleaning and replacing</i></c>

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.200 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>different equipment
within our substations.</i></c>

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.680 line:91%
<i>I haven't looked
at any point out</i>

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.640 line:91%
or down to see
how high I am.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.800 line:91%
I'm just kind of going,
there's the next bolt,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.400 line:85%
there's the next bolt.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.680 line:91%
And I'm aware that
the professionals

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.000 line:91%
...are doing this
much faster than me.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.920 line:85%
And I'm fine with that.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.920 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>We have a team
of 2,400 people</i></c>

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.200 line:85%
<c.yellow><i>that monitor and operate,</i></c>

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.080 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>maintain the network
around the clock, 24/7.</i></c>

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>This might be in response</c>

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.720 line:91%
<c.yellow>to weather or
faults on the network.</c>

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.400 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>And they keep
the energy flowing.</i></c>

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.600 line:91%
Presumably, you're
up here in all weathers.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.480 line:91%
You'll have dealt with
rain and snow.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.160 position:56% line:91%
<c.cyan>In an emergency,
they definitely work in bad weather.</c>

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.560 line:91%
<c.cyan>Can you just take
one more step up?</c>

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.520 line:85%
Yep.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.040 line:91%
<c.cyan>We'll attach you
onto the actual tower.</c>

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.720 line:91%
<c.cyan>Give you
a bit more space.</c>

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.680 line:85%
Thank you.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.080 line:91%
Oh, it's not a bad view
either, is it?

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.480 line:91%
<c.cyan>Yeah, it's
not bad around here.</c>

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.240 line:85%
So what, this is first arm.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.440 line:91%
<c.cyan>Yep, bottom phase,
middle phase, top phase.</c>

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.320 line:91%
Huh, it's different phase
of power on each arm?

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.160 line:91%
<c.cyan>Yep.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Right! That makes sense.</c>

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.320 line:91%
Because otherwise
the three would

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.360 line:91%
kind of interact with
each other on the wires.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.360 line:85%
<c.yellow>So we have 22,000 pylons.</c>

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.880 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>Each pylon weighs
approximately 30 tons.</i></c>

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.680 line:85%
<c.yellow><i>4,500 miles of overhead line,</i></c>

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.840 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>and 900 miles of
underground cable.</i></c>

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.360 line:91%
<c.cyan>Because we're
at our training school,</c>

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.080 line:91%
<c.cyan>we've got all different
combinations</c>

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.040 line:91%
<c.cyan>of wires in
different formations.</c>

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.920 line:85%
<c.cyan>We've got our new towers there.</c>

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.760 line:91%
<c.colorEEEEEE>Yeah, the T-pylons.</c>
<c.cyan>T-pylons, yep.</c>

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:40.880 line:85%
Which aren't as high.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.160 line:85%
<c.cyan>No, they're a bit lower.</c>

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.960 line:85%
I'll keep climbing.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.800 line:85%
Oh, it's getting real windy now!

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.800 line:85%
<c.cyan>You're doing good, Tom.</c>

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.200 line:0%
If you're wondering...

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.400 line:0%
this is the point where
the nerves kicked in.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.880 line:91%
I don't know if
that's coming across

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.400 line:85%
on microphone and camera, but...

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.920 line:85%
the wind is whistling

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.560 line:91%
through the metal and
through the wires.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.040 line:91%
<c.colorEEEEEE>Okay.</c>
<c.cyan>Well done, Tom.</c>

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.600 line:85%
<i>(laughs)</i><c.colorEEEEEE> Thank you.</c>

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.560 line:91%
<c.cyan>There's plenty of people
who don't get up here.</c>

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.080 line:85%
Mind the head.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.520 line:91%
Oh, how's that?
<i>(laughs)</i>

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.960 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>We own and
operate the network</i></c>

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>in England and Wales.</c>

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.120 line:91%
<c.yellow>We also operate
parts of Scotland.</c>

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.040 line:91%
<i>This training centre was built
in the heart of the country</i>

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.880 line:91%
<i>because a lot of the power
stations were around here,</i>

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.320 line:85%
<i>in the Midlands and Yorkshire.</i>

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.400 line:91%
<i>Because a lot of the coal
that fuelled those power stations</i>

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.360 line:91%
was being dug up
around here.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.400 line:91%
Not all of it – there was
plenty in Scotland

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.760 line:91%
and Tyneside and
South Wales too.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.640 line:91%
And a cluster of power
stations down on the Thames.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.720 line:91%
But if you were
going to pick a spot

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.400 line:91%
<i>in the centre
of the power grid</i>

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.880 line:91%
<i>to have the best access
of every pylon</i>

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.400 line:85%
...you'd pick here.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.720 line:85%
But things are changing,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.920 line:91%
and have been
changing, for decades.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.720 line:91%
The only coal-fired
station left in Britain

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:57.880 line:85%
is about an hour south

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:59.200 line:91%
<i>on the other side
of Nottingham,</i>

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.280 line:91%
<i>and that'll close down
within a year.</i>

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.720 line:91%
The main replacement
right now is gas,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.120 line:91%
and those stations tend
to be on the coast,

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.400 line:91%
where enormous tankers of
liquid natural gas can dock.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.040 line:91%
<i>The nuclear plants
are by the coast too,</i>

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.920 line:91%
<i>for easy access
to cooling water.</i>

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.200 line:85%
And then there's the wind.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.640 line:85%
Britain is a windswept island!

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.080 line:91%
<i>And we already
get a quarter</i>

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:21.720 line:91%
<i>of all our power
from the wind,</i>

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.920 position:58% line:91%
<i>with half of that from
colossal fields of turbines offshore.</i>

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.720 line:91%
<i>And there are more, and more,
and more of those being built.</i>

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.440 line:85%
Britain's entire power grid

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.480 line:91%
has been steadily turning
inside out for years.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.760 line:91%
As anyone who lives in a
former coal mining community

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.480 line:85%
like this knows all too well,

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.680 line:91%
power generation just isn't
happening here anymore.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.400 line:91%
Electricity isn't being
pushed out from the centre.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.480 line:91%
Instead, it's being
brought in from the coast.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.240 line:85%
Alright, next stop...

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.240 line:85%
Down on the wires.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.240 line:85%
I've got to be honest with you,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.760 line:91%
that ladder is swaying
a lot more than I'd like.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.600 line:91%
<c.cyan>Aye, I'm going to
go down first,</c>

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.240 line:91%
<c.cyan>and I'll get it a lot
more stable for you.</c>

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.480 line:85%
<i>(laughs)</i><c.colorEEEEEE> Thank you!</c>

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.000 line:85%
<c.yellow><i>National Grid's role is</i></c>

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.920 line:91%
<c.yellow>to move energy
around the country.</c>

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.320 line:85%
<c.yellow>We don't generate energy.</c>

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.040 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>We just connect it
to our network.</i></c>

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.440 line:91%
It's weird to see
all this infrastructure

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.280 line:85%
and not hear the bu—

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.560 line:91%
I mean, I guess
if you hear the buzzing

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.800 line:91%
and you're on here,
you're really in trouble.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.320 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>In total today,
we have 65 gigawatts</i></c>

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.960 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>of generation connected
to the network.</i></c>

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.480 line:85%
<c.yellow><i>That's a whole range of sources.</i></c>

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.240 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>So we have about
90 sources of generation.</i></c>

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.800 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>And the government has
a target to connect</i></c>

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.040 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>an additional 50 gigawatts
of offshore wind by 2030.</i></c>

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.760 line:91%
<c.colorEEEEEE>Oh!</c>
<c.cyan>Step down.</c>

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.920 line:91%
Okay. Like it's
any normal ladder.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.680 line:91%
A lot of metres
above the ground.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.680 line:85%
That's fine.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.360 line:91%
The ladder is angled
the wrong way.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.640 line:91%
My arms are further
back than my legs.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.680 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>In order to
connect the 50 gigawatts</i></c>

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.960 line:91%
<c.yellow>of offshore wind that
we're seeing out at sea,</c>

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.800 line:91%
<c.yellow>we need to create a
series of subsea cables,</c>

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.000 line:85%
<c.yellow>new overhead lines,</c>

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.440 line:91%
<c.yellow>in order to move that
energy across the network.</c>

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.040 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>As it stands, we know
that we need to construct</i></c>

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.880 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>five times as much
infrastructure</i></c>

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:59.080 line:91%
<c.yellow><i>in the next seven years
as we have in the last 30.</i></c>

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.880 line:91%
<c.cyan>So if you just
stop there for two seconds...</c>

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:04.040 line:91%
<c.colorEEEEEE>Yep.</c>
<c.cyan>You can stand on this.</c>

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.920 line:85%
<c.cyan>And it's pretty secure.</c>

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.440 line:85%
That was fun, okay.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.200 line:91%
<c.colorCCCCCC><i>(clattering)</i></c>
<i>(groans)</i>

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.320 line:85%
Okay, one foot on each wire.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.560 line:85%
<c.cyan>Wherever you feel comfortable,</c>

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.400 line:91%
<c.cyan>you're perfectly fine.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Alright.</c>

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:20.760 line:85%
Wow!

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.240 line:91%
<c.cyan>It's all
pretty sturdy stuff.</c>

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.280 line:91%
You didn't have to prove
that by bouncing though!

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.080 line:85%
<c.colorCCCCCC><i>(both laughing)</i></c>

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.600 line:91%
It's impossible to disentangle
politics from the power grid.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.520 line:91%
When the National Grid
was first built,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.920 line:85%
the government had to ignore

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.840 line:91%
so many complaints
to push it through.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.600 line:85%
But the benefits were obvious.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.760 line:91%
<i>And even the folks
who rallied against pylons</i>

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.640 line:91%
<i>would have to admit
that the result was</i>

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.920 line:91%
<i>probably worth it
compared to no electricity.</i>

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.240 line:91%
But now, and over
the next few years,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.880 line:91%
there are going to
be political fights

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.720 line:85%
and protests, large and small.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.800 line:91%
Because for the first time
in nearly a century,

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.800 line:91%
there are some big
and tough decisions

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.080 line:85%
to be made about pylons.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.560 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
